# Coding Standards

All implementation should follow:

## Human Written Code Standards

Code should look written by an experienced engineer.

Avoid:

• AI slop patterns
• Excessive abstraction
• Generic helpers
• Large generated files
• Duplicate logic
• Over engineering

Prefer:

• Clear naming
• Simple solutions
• Existing patterns
• Maintainable architecture

## Performance Standards

Consider:

• Time complexity
• Space complexity
• Database efficiency
• Network efficiency
• Rendering performance

Optimize when meaningful.

Do not sacrifice readability unnecessarily.

## Architecture Standards

Prefer:

• Separation of concerns
• High cohesion
• Low coupling
• Reusable abstractions
• Clear ownership

Avoid:

• God classes
• Giant components
• Circular dependencies
• Excessive global state

# Comment Standards

Generated code must follow:

• No comment longer than 3 lines
• Comments explain intent, not obvious code
• Comments improve semantic understanding
• No em dashes
• Remove stale comments
• Update outdated comments

# Anti Pattern Check

Do not introduce:

• Duplicate functionality
• Unnecessary abstractions
• Dead code
• Excessive dependencies
• Deep nesting
• Poor separation of concerns
• Breaking changes without justification
• Premature optimization
• Unnecessary state management
